#: ../../../macros.rst:5
msgid "Macros"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:6
msgid "Bust out your DSLRs, people. It is time to closely examine macros!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:9
msgid "What are macro instructions?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:10
msgid ""
"In generic terms, a programming macro is a special kind of syntax that "
"replaces a smaller amount of code with a larger expression, syntax tree, "
"code object, etc after the macro has been evaluated. In practice, macros "
"pause the normal parsing and evaluation of the code that they contain. This "
"is so that they can perform their expansion with a complete inputs. Roughly, "
"the algorithm executing a macro follows is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:17
msgid "Macro start, pause or skip normal parsing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:18
msgid "Gather macro inputs as strings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:19
msgid "Evaluate macro with inputs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:20
msgid "Resume normal parsing and execution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:22
msgid "Is this meta-programming? You betcha!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:25
msgid "When and where are macros used?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:26
msgid ""
"Macros are a practicality-beats-purity feature of many programming "
"languages. Because they allow you break out of the normal parsing cycle, "
"depending on the language, you can do some truly wild things with them. "
"However, macros are really there to reduce the amount of boiler plate code "
"that users and developers have to write."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:32
msgid ""
"In C and C++ (and Fortran), the C Preprocessor ``cpp`` is a macro evaluation "
"engine. For example, every time you see an ``#include`` or ``#ifdef``, this "
"is the ``cpp`` macro system in action. In these languages, the macros are "
"technically outside of the definition of the language at hand. Furthermore, "
"because ``cpp`` must function with only a single pass through the code, the "
"sorts of macros that can be written with ``cpp`` are relatively simple."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:40
msgid ""
"Rust, on the other hand, has a first-class notion of macros that look and "
"feel a lot like normal functions. Macros in Rust are capable of pulling off "
"type information from their arguments and preventing their return values "
"from being consumed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:45
msgid ""
"Other languages like Lisp, Forth, and Julia also provide their macro "
"systems. Even restructured text (rST) directives could be considered macros."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:48
msgid ""
"If these seem unfamiliar to the Python world, note that Jupyter and IPython "
"magics ``%`` and ``%%`` are macros!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:52
msgid "Function Macros"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:53
msgid ""
"Xonsh supports Rust-like macros that are based on normal Python callables. "
"Macros do not require a special definition in xonsh. However, like in Rust, "
"they must be called with an exclamation point ``!`` between the callable and "
"the opening parentheses ``(``. Macro arguments are split on the top-level "
"commas ``,``, like normal Python functions.  For example, say we have the "
"functions ``f`` and ``g``. We could perform a macro call on these functions "
"with the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:61
msgid ""
"# No macro args\n"
"f!()\n"
"\n"
"# Single arg\n"
"f!(x)\n"
"g!([y, 43, 44])\n"
"\n"
"# Two args\n"
"f!(x, x + 42)\n"
"g!([y, 43, 44], f!(z))"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:74
msgid ""
"Not so bad, right?  So what actually happens to the arguments when used in a "
"macro call?  Well, that depends on the definition of the function. In "
"particular, each argument in the macro call is matched up with the "
"corresponding parameter annotation in the callable's signature.  For "
"example, say we have an ``identity()`` function that annotates its sole "
"argument as a string:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:80
msgid ""
"def identity(x : str):\n"
"    return x"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:85
msgid ""
"If we call this normally, we'll just get whatever object we put in back out, "
"even if that object is not a string:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:88
msgid ""
"@ identity('me')\n"
"'me'\n"
"\n"
"@ identity(42)\n"
"42\n"
"\n"
"@ identity(identity)\n"
"<function __main__.identity>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:99
msgid ""
"However, if we perform macro calls instead we are now guaranteed to get the "
"string of the source code that is in the macro call:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:102
msgid ""
"@ identity!('me')\n"
"\"'me'\"\n"
"\n"
"@ identity!(42)\n"
"'42'\n"
"\n"
"@ identity!(identity)\n"
"'identity'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:113
msgid ""
"Also note that each macro argument is stripped prior to passing it to the "
"macro itself. This is done for consistency."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:116
msgid ""
"@ identity!(42)\n"
"'42'\n"
"\n"
"@ identity!(  42 )\n"
"'42'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:124
msgid ""
"Importantly, because we are capturing and not evaluating the source code, a "
"macro call can contain input that is beyond the usual syntax. In fact, that "
"is sort of the whole point. Here are some cases to start your gears turning:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:128
msgid ""
"@ identity!(import os)\n"
"'import os'\n"
"\n"
"@ identity!(if True:\n"
"@     pass)\n"
"'if True:\\n    pass'\n"
"\n"
"@ identity!(std::vector<std::string> x = {\"yoo\", \"hoo\"})\n"
"'std::vector<std::string> x = {\"yoo\", \"hoo\"}'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:140
msgid "You do you, ``identity()``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:143
msgid "Calling Function Macros"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:144
msgid ""
"There are a couple of points to consider when calling macros. The first is "
"that passing in arguments by name will not behave as expected. This is "
"because the ``<name>=`` is captured by the macro itself. Using the "
"``identity()`` function from above:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:149
msgid ""
"@ identity!(x=42)\n"
"'x=42'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:154
msgid "Performing a macro call uses only argument order to pass in values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:156
msgid ""
"Additionally, macro calls split arguments only on the top-level commas. The "
"top-level commas are not included in any argument. This behaves analogously "
"to normal Python function calls. For instance, say we have the following "
"``g()`` function that accepts two arguments:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:161
msgid ""
"def g(x : str, y : str):\n"
"    print('x = ' + repr(x))\n"
"    print('y = ' + repr(y))"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:167
msgid ""
"Then you can see the splitting and stripping behavior on each macro argument:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:170
msgid ""
"@ g!(42, 65)\n"
"x = '42'\n"
"y = '65'\n"
"\n"
"@ g!(42, 65,)\n"
"x = '42'\n"
"y = '65'\n"
"\n"
"@ g!( 42, 65, )\n"
"x = '42'\n"
"y = '65'\n"
"\n"
"@ g!(['x', 'y'], {1: 1, 2: 3})\n"
"x = \"['x', 'y']\"\n"
"y = '{1: 1, 2: 3}'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:188
msgid ""
"Sometimes you may only want to pass in the first few arguments as macro "
"arguments and you want the rest to be treated as normal Python arguments. By "
"convention, xonsh's macro caller will look for a lone ``*`` argument in "
"order to split the macro arguments and the regular arguments. So for example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:194
msgid ""
"@ g!(42, *, 65)\n"
"x = '42'\n"
"y = 65\n"
"\n"
"@ g!(42, *, y=65)\n"
"x = '42'\n"
"y = 65"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:204
msgid ""
"In the above, note that ``x`` is still captured as a macro argument. "
"However, everything after the ``*``, namely ``y``, is evaluated is if it "
"were passed in to a normal function call.  This can be useful for large "
"interfaces where only a handful of args are expected as macro arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:209
msgid "Hopefully, now you see the big picture."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:212
msgid "Writing Function Macros"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:213
msgid ""
"Though any function (or callable) can be used as a macro, this functionality "
"is probably most useful if the function was *designed* as a macro. There are "
"two main aspects of macro design to consider: argument annotations and call "
"site execution context."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:220
msgid "Macro Annotations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:221
msgid "There are six kinds of annotations that macros are able to interpret:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:223
msgid "Kinds of Annotation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:226
msgid "Category"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:227
msgid "Object"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:228
msgid "Flags"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:229
msgid "Modes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:230
msgid "Returns"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:231
msgid "String"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:232
msgid "``str``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:233
msgid "``'s'``, ``'str'``, or ``'string'``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:235
msgid "Source code of argument as string, *default*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:236
msgid "AST"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:237
msgid "``ast.AST``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:238
msgid "``'a'`` or ``'ast'``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:239 ../../../macros.rst:244
msgid "``'eval'`` (default), ``'exec'``, or ``'single'``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:240
msgid "Abstract syntax tree of argument."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:241
msgid "Code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:242
msgid "``types.CodeType`` or ``compile``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:243
msgid "``'c'``, ``'code'``, or ``'compile'``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:245
msgid "Compiled code object of argument."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:246
msgid "Eval"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:247
msgid "``eval`` or ``None``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:248
msgid "``'v'`` or ``'eval'``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:250
msgid "Evaluation of the argument."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:251
msgid "Exec"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:252
msgid "``exec``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:253
msgid "``'x'`` or ``'exec'``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:254
msgid "``'exec'`` (default) or ``'single'``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:255
msgid "Execs the argument and returns None."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:256
msgid "Type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:257
msgid "``type``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:258
msgid "``'t'`` or ``'type'``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:260
msgid "The type of the argument after it has been evaluated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:262
msgid ""
"These annotations allow you to hook into whichever stage of the compilation "
"that you desire. It is important to note that the string form of the "
"arguments is split and stripped (as described above) prior to conversion to "
"the annotation type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:267
msgid ""
"Each argument may be annotated with its own individual type. Annotations may "
"be provided as either objects or as the string flags seen in the above "
"table. String flags are case-insensitive. If an argument does not have an "
"annotation, ``str`` is selected. This makes the macro function call behave "
"like the subprocess macros and context manager macros below. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:274
msgid ""
"def func(a, b : 'AST', c : compile):\n"
"    pass"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:279
msgid "In a macro call of ``func!()``,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:281
msgid "``a`` will be evaluated with ``str`` since no annotation was provided,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:282
msgid "``b`` will be parsed into a syntax tree node, and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:283
msgid ""
"``c`` will be compiled into code object since the builtin ``compile()`` "
"function was used as the annotation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:286
msgid ""
"Additionally, certain kinds of annotations have different modes that affect "
"the parsing, compilation, and execution of its argument.  While a sensible "
"default is provided, you may also supply your own. This is done by "
"annotating with a (kind, mode) tuple.  The first element can be any valid "
"object or flag. The second element must be a corresponding mode as a "
"string.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:293
msgid ""
"def gunc(d : (exec, 'single'), e : ('c', 'exec')):\n"
"    pass"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:298
msgid "Thus in a macro call of ``gunc!()``,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:300
msgid "``d`` will be exec'd in single-mode (rather than exec-mode), and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:301
msgid "``e`` will be compiled in exec-mode (rather than eval-mode)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:303
msgid ""
"For more information on the differences between the exec, eval, and single "
"modes please see the Python documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:308
msgid "Macro Function Execution Context"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:309
msgid ""
"Equally important as having the macro arguments is knowing the execution "
"context of the macro call itself. Rather than mucking around with frames, "
"macros provide both the globals and locals of the call site.  These are "
"accessible as the ``macro_globals`` and ``macro_locals`` attributes of the "
"macro function itself while the macro is being executed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:315
msgid ""
"For example, consider a macro which replaces all literal ``1`` digits with "
"the literal ``2``, evaluates the modification, and returns the results. To "
"eval, the macro will need to pull off its globals and locals:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:319
msgid ""
"def one_to_two(x : str):\n"
"    s = x.replace('1', '2')\n"
"    glbs = one_to_two.macro_globals\n"
"    locs = one_to_two.macro_locals\n"
"    return eval(s, glbs, locs)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:327
msgid "Running this with a few of different inputs, we see:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:329
msgid ""
"@ one_to_two!(1 + 1)\n"
"4\n"
"\n"
"@ one_to_two!(11)\n"
"22\n"
"\n"
"@ x = 1\n"
"@ one_to_two!(x + 1)\n"
"3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:341
msgid ""
"Of course, many other more sophisticated options are available depending on "
"the use case."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:346
msgid "Subprocess Macros"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:347
msgid ""
"Like with function macros above, subprocess macros allow you to pause the "
"parser for until you are ready to exit subprocess mode. Unlike function "
"macros, there is only a single macro argument and its macro type is always a "
"string.  This is because it (usually) doesn't make sense to pass non-string "
"arguments to a command. And when it does, there is the ``@()`` syntax!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:353
msgid ""
"In the simplest case, subprocess macros look like the equivalent of their "
"function macro counterparts:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:356
msgid ""
"@ echo! I'm Mr. Meeseeks.\n"
"I'm Mr. Meeseeks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:361
msgid ""
"Again, note that everything to the right of the ``!`` is passed down to the "
"``echo`` command as the final, single argument. This is space preserving, "
"like wrapping with quotes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:365
msgid ""
"# normally, xonsh will split on whitespace,\n"
"# so each argument is passed in separately\n"
"@ echo x  y       z\n"
"x y z\n"
"\n"
"# usually space can be preserved with quotes\n"
"@ echo \"x  y       z\"\n"
"x  y       z\n"
"\n"
"# however, subprocess macros will pause and then strip\n"
"# all input after the exclamation point\n"
"@ echo! x  y       z\n"
"x  y       z"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:381
msgid ""
"However, the macro will pause everything, including path and environment "
"variable expansion, that might be present even with quotes.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:384
msgid ""
"# without macros, environment variable are expanded\n"
"@ echo $USER\n"
"lou\n"
"\n"
"# inside of a macro, all additional munging is turned off.\n"
"@ echo! $USER\n"
"$USER"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:394
msgid ""
"Everything to the right of the exclamation point, except the leading and "
"trailing whitespace, is passed into the command directly as written. This "
"allows certain commands to function in cases where quoting or piping might "
"be more burdensome. The ``timeit`` command is a great example where simple "
"syntax will often fail, but will be easily executable as a macro:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:400
msgid ""
"# fails normally\n"
"@ timeit \"hello mom \" + \"and dad\"\n"
"xonsh: subprocess mode: command not found: hello\n"
"\n"
"# macro success!\n"
"@ timeit! \"hello mom \" + \"and dad\"\n"
"100000000 loops, best of 3: 8.24 ns per loop"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:410
msgid ""
"All expressions to the left of the exclamation point are passed in normally "
"and are not treated as the special macro argument. This allows the mixing of "
"simple and complex command line arguments. For example, sometimes you might "
"really want to write some code in another language:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:415
msgid ""
"# don't worry, it is temporary!\n"
"@ bash -c ! export var=42; echo $var\n"
"42\n"
"\n"
"# that's better!\n"
"@ python -c ! import os; print(os.path.abspath(\"/\"))\n"
"/"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:425
msgid ""
"Compared to function macros, subprocess macros are relatively simple. "
"However, they can still be very expressive!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:429
msgid "Context Manager Macros"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:430
msgid ""
"Now that we have seen what life can be like with macro expressions, it is "
"time to introduce the macro statement: ``with!``.  With-bang provides macros "
"on top of existing Python context managers. This provides both anonymous and "
"onymous blocks in xonsh."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:435
msgid ""
"The syntax for context manager macros is the same as the usual with-"
"statement in Python, but with an additional exclamation point between the "
"``with`` word and the first context manager expression. As a simple example,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:439
msgid ""
"with! x:\n"
"    y = 10\n"
"    print(y)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:445
msgid ""
"In the above, everything to the left of the colon (``x``) will be evaluated "
"normally. However, the body will not be executed and ``y`` will not be "
"defined or printed. In this case, the body will be attached to x as a "
"string, along with globals and locals, prior to the body even being entered. "
"The body is then replaced with a ``pass`` statement. You can think of the "
"above as being transformed into the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:452
msgid ""
"x.macro_block = 'y = 10\\nprint(y)\\n'\n"
"x.macro_globals = globals()\n"
"x.macro_locals = locals()\n"
"with! x:\n"
"    pass"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:460
msgid "There are a few important things about this to notice:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:462
msgid "The ``macro_block`` string is dedented,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:463
msgid ""
"The ``macro_*`` attributes are set *before* the context manager is entered "
"so the ``__enter__()`` method may use them, and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:465
msgid ""
"The ``macro_*`` attributes are not cleaned up automatically so that the "
"context manager may use them even after the object is exited. The "
"``__exit__()`` method may clean up these attributes, if desired."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:469
msgid ""
"By default, macro blocks are returned as a string. However, like with "
"function macro arguments, the kind of ``macro_block`` is determined by a "
"special annotation.  This annotation is given via the ``__xonsh_block__`` "
"attribute on the context manager itself.  This allows the block to be "
"interpreted as an AST, byte compiled, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:475
msgid ""
"The convenient part about this syntax is that the macro block is only exited "
"once it sees a dedent back to the level of the ``with!``. All other code is "
"indiscriminately skipped! This allows you to write blocks of code in "
"languages other than xonsh without pause."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:480
msgid ""
"For example, consider a simple XML macro context manager. This will return "
"the parsed XML tree from a macro block. The context manager itself can be "
"written as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:485
msgid ""
"import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET\n"
"\n"
"class XmlBlock:\n"
"\n"
"    # make sure the macro_block comes back as a string\n"
"    __xonsh_block__ = str\n"
"\n"
"    def __enter__(self):\n"
"        # parse and return the block on entry\n"
"        root = ET.fromstring(self.macro_block)\n"
"        return root\n"
"\n"
"    def __exit__(self, *exc):\n"
"        # no reason to keep these attributes around.\n"
"        del self.macro_block, self.macro_globals, self.macro_locals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:504
msgid "The above class may then be used in a with-bang as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:506
msgid ""
"with! XmlBlock() as tree:\n"
"    <note>\n"
"      <to>You</to>\n"
"      <from>Xonsh</from>\n"
"      <heading>Don't You Want Me, Baby</heading>\n"
"      <body>\n"
"        You know I don't believe you when you say that you don't need me.\n"
"      </body>\n"
"    </note>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:518
msgid ""
"And if you run this, you'll see that the ``tree`` object really is a parsed "
"XML object."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:521
msgid ""
"@ print(tree.tag)\n"
"note"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:527
msgid ""
"So in roughly eight lines of xonsh code, you can seamlessly interface with "
"another, vastly different language."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:530
msgid ""
"The possibilities for this are not limited to just markup languages or other "
"party tricks. You could be a remote execution interface via SSH, RPC, dask / "
"distributed, etc. The real benefit of context manager macros is that they "
"allow you to select when, where, and what code is executed as a part of the "
"xonsh language itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:536
msgid "The power is there; use it without reservation!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:539
msgid "Subprocess Expression Macro ``@!()``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:540
msgid ""
"The ``@!()`` operator captures its content as a **literal string** and "
"passes it as a single argument in subprocess mode. Unlike ``@()`` which "
"evaluates the expression at runtime, ``@!()`` preserves the source text as-"
"is."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:544
msgid ""
"@ echo @!(2+2)\n"
"2+2\n"
"\n"
"@ echo @(2+2)\n"
"4"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:552
msgid ""
"The content can be any text -- Python expressions, shell commands, or "
"arbitrary strings:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:555
msgid ""
"@ echo @!(x if x > 0 else -x)\n"
"x if x > 0 else -x\n"
"\n"
"@ echo @!(ls -la /tmp)\n"
"ls -la /tmp\n"
"\n"
"@ echo @!(f\"{name}: {value}\")\n"
"f\"{name}: {value}\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:566
msgid "Syntax highlighting and autocompletion work inside ``@!()``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:568
msgid "**Example: xontrib-pipeliner**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:570
msgid ""
"The `xontrib-pipeliner <https://github.com/anki-code/xontrib-pipeliner>`_ "
"``pl`` command takes a Python expression as a string argument and applies it "
"to each line of input. ``@!()`` is a natural fit:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:574
msgid ""
"@ echo 123 | pl @!(line + '!!!')\n"
"123!!!\n"
"\n"
"@ ls | pl @!(line.upper())\n"
"\n"
"@ import json  # import any library\n"
"  cat data.json | pl @!(json.loads(line))  # process using that library"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:584
msgid ""
"Without ``@!()``, you would need to quote the expression manually, which is "
"error-prone when the expression itself contains quotes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../macros.rst:587
msgid ""
"# quoting gets messy:\n"
"@ echo 123 | pl \"line + '!!!'\"\n"
"\n"
"# @!() just works:\n"
"@ echo 123 | pl @!(line + '!!!')\n"
"\n"
"@ $(@lines xonsh -c @!(echo hello; echo world))"
msgstr ""

